WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.400
Which of our applications has no
owner and no maintenance budget?

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.990
Four ways software fails
before anyone attacks it.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.300
First, keys typed into the code.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.530
That code is copied to every laptop,

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.350
the build server, backups and agencies.

00:00:16.795 --> 00:00:21.585
If the repository goes public by accident,
bots find the keys within minutes.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.440
Keys committed years
ago usually still work.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:26.880
Second, the build pipeline.

00:00:27.435 --> 00:00:31.345
A chain of machines compiles,
tests and publishes every release.

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:34.120
It runs unattended, with broad rights.

00:00:34.455 --> 00:00:37.065
An attacker adds a few
lines during the build.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.430
The result is signed with our certificate,

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.310
and customers trust it.

00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:45.093
Third, security tested once,
after the fact.

00:00:45.943 --> 00:00:50.523
A penetration test months after release
finds flaws that were cheap to fix during

00:00:50.523 --> 00:00:51.113
design.

00:00:51.553 --> 00:00:55.443
Now the code is live, and the flaws
were reachable the whole time.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:58.600
Fourth, software behaves like a pet.

00:00:58.885 --> 00:01:00.115
The agency delivers,

00:01:00.585 --> 00:01:04.155
the contract ends,
and nobody is assigned to the application.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:07.740
Components age,
and advisories pile up unread.

00:01:08.025 --> 00:01:11.195
The portal still holds every
customer record it collected.

00:01:12.283 --> 00:01:13.493
What good looks like.

00:01:13.778 --> 00:01:15.408
Secret scanning and a vault.

00:01:16.288 --> 00:01:16.818
A protected pipeline:

00:01:17.468 --> 00:01:20.338
MFA, least privilege and signed builds.

00:01:20.458 --> 00:01:23.968
Security in the definition of done,
with automated checks.

00:01:24.358 --> 00:01:27.408
A named owner and a budget
for every application.

00:01:27.773 --> 00:01:31.443
NIS2 names security in
development and maintenance,

00:01:32.073 --> 00:01:35.403
and the OWASP Top Ten is the
yardstick for the checks.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:38.540
A suggested first step.

00:01:38.825 --> 00:01:41.275
Ask for the list of
applications in production.

00:01:41.715 --> 00:01:44.285
Mark the ones with no owner and no budget.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:47.900
That list is the real risk
register for this topic.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.020
Four ways software fails.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:54.080
Every application needs
an owner and a budget.

